/* PATTERNS: the ideal structure per archetype. The site shows the pattern, the Dossier does the sequencing. */
ARCH.exec.pattern={goal:'Cut the marginal rate on employment income without touching the equity you have already earned.',structure:'There is no entity play. Salary is taxed where you work, so residence is the only lever, and the lever is an inbound regime on employment income. Italy exempts half your salary for 5 years, more with children. Spain taxes new arrivals at a flat 24% to €600K for 6 years. France exempts 30% for 8. Portugal charges 20% on qualifying roles. Switzerland is a question of canton. Dubai is zero, Singapore caps at 24%, Hong Kong at 15%.',trap:'Equity. Options and RSUs granted before the move stay partly sourced to the old country and are taxed there on exercise or vesting, however far you have gone.',dossier:'Which regime fits the contract, and the date the contract must be signed relative to the date residence starts.'};
ARCH.founder.pattern={goal:'Sell at zero or close to it, and leave the old country without an exit tax bill.',structure:'Hold the shares personally in a jurisdiction that does not tax private capital gains, Switzerland, Singapore, Hong Kong, the UAE, Monaco, Belgium, New Zealand, or through a holding with a participation exemption if a company is the seller, Luxembourg, the Netherlands, a Swiss AG. Move before the term sheet, not after.',trap:'Exit tax on the way out. Germany taxes unrealized gains on holdings of 1% or more when you leave, France above €800K, Spain above €4M, Norway and the Netherlands too, Canada on everything. The UK has no exit tax but claws the gain back if you return within 5 tax years. Earn outs and post sale employment are taxed where you work them.',dossier:'The exit rules of your specific old country, the residence date the sale must sit behind, and whether the shares move into a holding first.'};
ARCH.investor.pattern={goal:'Low tax on dividends, interest, and gains, in a place where inheritance does not take 40%.',structure:'A fixed fee regime or a zero seat. The Swiss lump sum, taxed on living costs with wealth tax still applying. Italy at €200K a year on all foreign income, Greece at €100K. Cyprus non dom, zero on dividends and interest for 17 years. Malta at 15% on remitted income. The UK for 4 years of foreign income. Dubai, Monaco, the Bahamas, and Cayman at zero. Assets sit in a fund vehicle or a private trust company for succession, not in your own name.',trap:'At this stage wealth tax and inheritance tax outweigh income tax. Geneva takes near 1% of net assets a year, Zug near 0.2%, Spain and Norway both tax wealth. The UK reaches 40% of your estate once you have been resident 10 years, France 45%. Italy charges 4% above €1M per child, Portugal and most Swiss cantons nothing to a spouse or children.',dossier:'The wealth tax and inheritance arithmetic on your actual balance sheet, which reorders the ranking for anyone above about $20M.'};
ARCH.builder.pattern={goal:'A low personal rate and a company taxed where it is run, with enough substance that the old country cannot claim it.',structure:'Residence in a territorial or low tax seat, and a company managed and controlled from there: board, decisions, and an office in the same place. A UAE free zone company plus residence. A Cyprus company at 12.5% plus non dom status. A Singapore Pte Ltd with the startup exemptions. Malta refunded down to an effective 5%. Portugal through IFICI if the role qualifies.',trap:'Place of effective management. If the decisions are still made from the old kitchen table, or the team sits in the old country, the old country taxes the company as its own. A remote work visa gives you a permit, not a tax residence, and the 183 day count keeps running.',dossier:'The substance test for your old country, and your staff map.'};
const OVERLAYS=[{k:'us',t:'If you carry a US passport',p:'The US taxes citizens wherever they live, so every structure above cuts local tax and leaves federal tax standing. The one seat that changes federal exposure without renouncing is Puerto Rico under Act 60, zero on Puerto Rico sourced gains and 4% corporate for real residents. Renouncing triggers the expatriation tax above $2M of net worth. Plan around the floor, not around zero.'},{k:'family',t:'If you are moving a family',p:'For a family the inheritance table outranks the income tax table. No inheritance tax: Australia, Canada, Singapore, Hong Kong, the UAE, Austria, Monaco and Portugal in the direct line, most Swiss cantons to a spouse and children. Heavy: Japan 55%, Korea 50%, France 45%, the UK 40%, the US 40% above the exemption, Germany to 30%. Forced heirship in France, Italy, and Japan decides who gets what before your will does. The wrapper is chosen for the children, not for you.'}];
